For arbitrary positive logical activities, sample from the bag matching law and reveal the edge portions as above. The all-real event has probability $1/I$; conditional on this event, the decoded logical matching has law $\pi_\lambda$. For each logical edge $uv$, $$\label{eq:probe}
 \mathbb P(\text{probe for }uv\text{ is used and all other edges are real})
 =\frac{g_\lambda(uv)}{DI}.$$ Conditional on this event, the residual logical matching has the $\lambda$-weighted perfect-matching law on the vertices other than $u,v$.

Let $B\ge1$ be a symmetric similarity on a finite vertex set $X$, satisfying $B_{ij}\ge\min\{B_{ik},B_{kj}\}$. Let $\mathcal C_t$ be the equivalence classes of $B\ge t$, including singletons. For every even set $U\subseteq X$, $$\label{eq:threshold-pairing}
 \log\Phi_B(U)
 =\int_1^\infty\sum_{C\in\mathcal C_t}
       \left\lfloor\frac{|C\cap U|}{2}\right\rfloor\frac{dt}{t}.$$ There is one pairing that attains the maximum number of internal pairs in every threshold class simultaneously.

For every two real functions $f_1,f_2$ on $\mathcal M$, $$\label{eq:pair-energy}
 \mathop{\mathrm{Var}}_\pi f_1\le L\,
 \mathcal E\bigl((A,B)\mapsto f_1(A)+f_2(B)\bigr).$$ The same constant works for every common-interval clamp of the reference activities.

With the notation above, $$\begin{aligned}
 \sum_A\pi(A)\sum_{A'\in\mathcal S(A)}
       (f_1(A)-f_1(A'))^2
 &\le60N^2A_0^2 E,
 \label{eq:safe-switch-energy}\\
 \sum_A\pi(A)\sum_{A'\in\mathcal K(A)}
       (f_1(A)-f_1(A'))^2
 &\le12NA_0 E,
 \label{eq:color-energy}\\
 \sum_{A,B}\pi(A)\pi(B)\sum_{C\in\mathcal C(A,B)}
       \bigl(F(A,B)-F((A,B)^C)\bigr)^2
 &\le6NE.
 \label{eq:cycle-energy}
\end{aligned}$$ Here $\mathcal C(A,B)$ denotes the discrepancy cycles, and $(A,B)^C$ is the pair after swapping $C$.

The quantities just defined satisfy $$\label{eq:single-cell-identity}
 D_C=\Delta+\sum_{J\in P}g_J-\sum_{J\in Q}g_J+E_P-E_Q$$ for each cell. Consequently, $$\label{eq:global-cell-identity}
 D_C=\sum_{\text{cells of }C}(\Delta+E_P-E_Q).$$

Suppose the current logical activities are balanced and every empirical mean is within $\sigma$ of its exact mean. Then the next scaled activities are balanced and $$\label{eq:stage-relative-error}
 \left|\frac{\widehat R}{R}-1\right|
 \le10\sigma\le\frac\varepsilon{100K}.$$
